#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100638 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e092e191-84ac-5d9f-b0db-94d628a6154e" :question/math-academy-id "e-525" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny' = -\\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad x \\neq 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f'(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$\n\n- Substituting $y = f(x)$ and $y' = f'(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ and $y' = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100708 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82a9b23c-4c79-56cd-9761-79d0ee22646d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Initial Value Problems"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5ffa549-e77e-5db4-9fde-5b0134957539" :question/math-academy-id "e-8732" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{3y}{x}, \\qquad y(-1) = -2.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-1) = -2.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}{-1}}) = {\\color{blue}{-2}}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{x}^{3} \\\\ -2 & =A(-1)^{3} \\\\ -2 & =A(-1) \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 2x^3 .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100714 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1228900d-87d4-5ff0-8e01-ff9417e82220" :question/math-academy-id "e-9978" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y = e^x + 1$\n\n- $y = -e^x$\n\n- $y = 2e^x$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y = e^x + 1,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ {e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}{e}^{x}+1 \\\\ 0 & \\ne 1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y = e^x+1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = -e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ -{e}^{x} & =-{e}^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = -e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = 2e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ 2{e}^{x} & =2{e}^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = 2e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b393b878-c5bd-525b-95d3-a6f539ad09f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098191} {:answer/id #uuid "cc57abfc-d7d4-5bbe-b820-47d6bcfddc83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098192} {:answer/id #uuid "f102d687-4edb-5d57-a6d8-6e06a4e9175f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098193} {:answer/id #uuid "01ed849a-83c5-5a2e-9479-ecd16db6de49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098194} {:answer/id #uuid "40cebea2-5d0d-5d53-9583-e01b08ec8cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40cebea2-5d0d-5d53-9583-e01b08ec8cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098195} :answer-field/id #uuid "23c7473c-7e8a-5122-8097-f7924bdb001a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47b66471-b70f-5509-8368-6a6674b74447" :question/math-academy-id "q-141078" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)={e}^{2x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)={e}^{2x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({e}^{2x})}^{′} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}^{′}=2{e}^{2x}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}2y \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3f7892e-2376-5f4d-ab2f-2fc9850117bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281414} {:answer/id #uuid "c58e99f4-4cc4-5dd5-b7e6-5456542cb904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281415} {:answer/id #uuid "91627b8a-627f-5c2e-a6ff-0ff3d52ce635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281416} {:answer/id #uuid "24d20f25-8bb9-5064-94e6-5bca9c6df55b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281417} {:answer/id #uuid "1aea66b2-c4cb-5cf9-b060-bc56cab96f27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1aea66b2-c4cb-5cf9-b060-bc56cab96f27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281418} :answer-field/id #uuid "14ce3d7f-290b-5336-8578-d6aa19601a4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78b7d940-8463-5444-b751-5735936b4729" :question/math-academy-id "q-141093" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}}.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{x}}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ 1 & =2\\sqrt{x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & =\\frac{2}{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true.* Therefore, the function $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-1/2})}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{x}^{-1/2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & =2\\sqrt{x} \\\\ -1 & =2x\\sqrt{x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8eb959c-4147-5c9f-87ec-004b09f3501f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281428} {:answer/id #uuid "22c8ebdf-8e74-5323-9672-5c39508def7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281429} {:answer/id #uuid "27b529fd-6543-558f-9aeb-7d399d8bf7da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281430} {:answer/id #uuid "625d9350-740f-5374-b637-d8e7c15778af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281431} {:answer/id #uuid "582b77a2-82b2-521f-aa21-1eea403a5828" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8eb959c-4147-5c9f-87ec-004b09f3501f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281428} :answer-field/id #uuid "75912ee8-4ef3-5ab6-ad3b-7e543bef68c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06bd8f05-4440-5830-a789-d3d0eb85823a" :question/math-academy-id "q-141099" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$\n- $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\frac{1}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(-\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\ne \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true.* Therefore, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}} & =-\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true.* Therefore, the function $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8eda923f-1829-5e8c-88f7-3943b21e23fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281435} {:answer/id #uuid "e99ba2b9-eed2-57d8-8720-a71caa9e2f60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281436} {:answer/id #uuid "47d9e7d0-433c-50c0-b8b8-873dc870e499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281437} {:answer/id #uuid "edadaffd-dff1-5ac4-bcbd-3ab12dd79405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281438} {:answer/id #uuid "80ce65d4-a0f0-5dcf-a74a-2943f8ee9d4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47d9e7d0-433c-50c0-b8b8-873dc870e499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281437} :answer-field/id #uuid "d70d9c7f-1be6-57d6-9f0a-ad943b684745" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "482773e5-5b8a-5acd-8d88-a970f8a254f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-141103" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}}.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{3(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-3})}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{x}^{-3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & ={x}^{3} \\\\ -1 & ={x}^{4}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{3(-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-6})}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{x}^{-6}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & ={x}^{6} \\\\ -2 & ={x}^{7}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82a9b23c-4c79-56cd-9761-79d0ee22646d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cf997eb-6058-5c08-a5f0-d2d144aba426" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cf997eb-6058-5c08-a5f0-d2d144aba426" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281455} :answer-field/id #uuid "07c72c27-a9d0-5f4d-9333-493f99092f6f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53d754a0-a4b9-5c1a-ac3a-5eea26512dd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-352655" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4y,\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{e}^{-4x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=A{e}^{-4x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(1)=2.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(1)=2$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{-4x} \\\\ 2 & =A{e}^{-4\\cdot 1} \\\\ 2 & =A{e}^{-4} \\\\ 2{e}^{4} & =A \\\\ A & =2{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b5098d2-8759-5c88-873b-59c47317c676" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100303} {:answer/id #uuid "bef0901a-3e08-5d64-8008-69c96233a746" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100304} {:answer/id #uuid "ef60a1c2-3509-51f2-bca5-8747b265f89a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100305} {:answer/id #uuid "1164be35-3601-5d59-bd65-4712ef249554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100306} {:answer/id #uuid "14f11516-8f03-5153-b333-a0fbd914b758" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1164be35-3601-5d59-bd65-4712ef249554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100306} :answer-field/id #uuid "917fb1fb-b6b6-5fde-815a-f160d6a1eef4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a773fc2b-812d-5f5c-8cbb-9b1672c48928" :question/math-academy-id "q-79335" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y}{x}-2,\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=2x$\n- $y={x}^{2}$\n- $y={x}^{3}$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=2x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y}{x}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot 2x}{x}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{x}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\overset{?}{=}4-2 \\\\ 2 & =2.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=2x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{2},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y}{x}-2 \\\\ 2x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot {x}^{2}}{x}-2 \\\\ 2x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x}-2 \\\\ 2x & \\overset{?}{=}2x-2 \\\\ 0 & \\ne -2.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y={x}^{2}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{3},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y}{x}-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot {x}^{3}}{x}-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{x}-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}2{x}^{2}-2 \\\\ {x}^{2} & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y={x}^{3}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}]}
